n give is \\( n - 1 \\). Setting the condition \\( \\binom{n}{2} > 200 \\), leads us to \\( \\frac{n(n-1)}{2} > 200 \\).\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n-1)}{2} > 200 \n   \\]\n   \n   Multiply both sides by 2:\n\n   \\[\n   n(n-1) > 400\n   \\]\n\n3. **Solve the quadratic inequality**:\n\n   We now need to solve the quadratic inequality \\( n(n-1) > 400 \\):\n\n   \\[\n   n^2 - n - 400 > 0\n   \\]\n\n   This is a quadratic inequality and can be solved by finding the roots of the corresponding equation:\n\n   \\[\n   n^2 - n - 400 = 0\n   \\]\n\n   Solve for \\( n \\):\n\n   \\[\n   n = \\frac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{1 + 4 \\times 400}}{2}\n   \\]\n   \n   \\[\n   n = \\frac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{1601}}{2}\n   \\]\n   \n   Approximate the values:\n\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{1601} \\approx 40.0125 \\implies n \\approx \\frac{1 + 40.0125}{2} \\approx 20.506\n   \\]\n\n   Hence, the smallest integer \\( n \\) satisfying this equation is \\( n = 21 \\).\n\n4. **Validation for \\( n = 20 \\)**:\n\n    For \\( n = 20 \\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{20 \\times 19}{2} = 190 \\quad (\\text{190 pairs})\n   \\]\n\n   Since 190 < 200, it's possible to distribute candies such that no two students have the same number of candies.\n\n5. **Validation for \\( n = 21 \\)**:\n\n    For \\( n = 21 \\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{21 \\times 20}{2} = 210 \\quad (\\text{210 pairs})\n   \\]\n\n   Since 210 > 200, there will always be at least one pair of students with the same number of candies.\n\n### Conclusion\nThus, the minimum number of students required to ensure that at least two will receive the same number of candies is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{21}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "In the given triangle \\( \\triangle ABC \\) inscribed in circle \\( O \\), point \\( P \\) is located on arc \\( BC \\). Point \\( K \\) lies on \\( AP \\) such that \\( BK \\) bisects \\( \\angle ABC \\). Circle \\( \\Omega \\) passes through points \\( K \\), \\( P \\), and \\( C \