u might still be able to force a win by moving to a position that is a losing position for the opponent.\n\nSo let's try to define the positions step by step.\n\nFirst, label all positions that can reach (8,8) in one move as winning positions. These are (7,8), (8,7), (7,7). Then, for other positions, check if they can move to a losing position.\n\nBut to determine losing positions, we need to find positions where all possible moves lead to winning positions. So starting from the positions adjacent to the winning positions.\n\nWait, maybe another way is to model this as a DAG and compute Grundy numbers for each position. The Grundy number is the mex (minimum excludant) of the Grundy numbers of the positions reachable from it. If the Grundy number is 0, it's a losing position; otherwise, it's a winning position.\n\nLet me recall: mex(S) is the smallest non-negative integer not in S. So for each position, compute mex of the Grundy numbers of its neighbors.\n\nBut in this game, the moves are to the right, up, or diagonal up-right. So the movement is only increasing in i and/or j. Therefore, the graph is a DAG with edges only going towards higher i or j. Hence, we can compute Grundy numbers starting from (8,8) backward.\n\nBut wait, (8,8) is the terminal position. If we reach it, the game ends. So in Grundy number terms, the terminal position has Grundy number 0 because it's an ending position with no moves. However, in our case, moving to (8,8) is a winning move. Therefore, perhaps the positions adjacent to (8,8) have Grundy number 1, and so on.\n\nBut I need to think carefully. In standard impartial games, the Grundy number represents equivalence to nim heaps. If a position has Grundy number 0, it's a losing position; otherwise, it's a winning position.\n\nSo, in our case, the terminal position (8,8) is not a position you can move from, so it's like a terminal with no moves. Hence, its Grundy number is 0. Then, positions that can move to (8,8) would have Grundy number 