gonal path, leveraging the odd minimal move count. This strategy ensures victory regardless of the second player\u2019s attempts to prolong the game. \n\n**Final Answer**:  \nThe first player can force a win by always moving diagonally when possible. Thus, with optimal play, the starting player wins.  \n\n$\\boxed{\\text{First player wins}}$", "ground_truth_solution": "1. **Identify Winning and Losing Positions:**\n   - We start by analyzing the end goal, which is the top-right corner of the chessboard.\n   - Let us denote the top-right corner square as the target square.\n   - The positions that are one move away from the target square are winning positions because the current player can immediately move the piece into the target square and win the game. These squares are the ones immediately to the left, directly below, and diagonally below-left of the target square. We label these squares as \"1.\"\n\n2. **Marking Losing Positions:**\n   - From these winning positions, we can find the losing positions for the opponent.\n   - A losing position is any position where all possible moves would lead to a winning position for the opponent.\n   - For example, if the piece is on a square from where any move puts it on a \"1\"-marked square (winning position), then this square is a losing position. Mark these squares as \"2.\"\n\n3. **Iterate for Further Positions:**\n   - Repeat the process for identifying winning and losing positions:\n     - Squares from which any move leads to a \"2\" (losing position) are winning positions, and we mark them with \"3.\"\n     - Squares from which any move leads to a \"3\" (winning position) are losing positions, and we mark them with \"4.\"\n   - Continue this process until all squares on the board are marked appropriately as either winning or losing positions. Refer to the diagram to encode this.\n\n4. **Determine the Starting Position:**\n   - The lower-left corner of the board is the starting position of the piece.\n   - Check the marking of this starting position in the completed chessboard diagram. If it is a marked winning square, the player who starts the game can always win with the correct strategy.\n\n5. **Strategy for the First Player:**\n   - Ensure the opponent always finds themselves on a losing position.\n   - One effective strategy for the first player is to move diagonally up and to the right on their first move.\n   - Afterward, the first player mimics the opponent's move (if the opponent moves up, the first player does the same; if the opponent moves to the right, the first player does the same). This ensures that the opponent always ends up on a losing position.\n\n6. **Conclusion:**\n   - Since the starting position is marked as a winning square based on our backward induction process, the first player can always enforce a strategy that forces the opponent to be on losing positions continuously.\n   - Thus, following optimal play, the first player will always win.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The starter will always win if they play optimally.}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "We draw \\( n \\) lines in the plane, with no two of them being parallel and no three of them being concurrent. Into how many regions is the plane divided?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to figure out how many regions the plane is divided into when we draw n lines, with no two parallel and no three meeting at the same point. Hmm, let's start by visualizing some simple cases. Maybe if I start with small values of n, I can spot a pattern.\n\nLet's see, when n=0, there are no lines, so the plane is just one region. That makes sense. Now, if I draw one line (n=1), it divides the plane into two regions. Right, two halves. For n=2, since the lines aren't parallel, they intersect once. So each new line intersects the previous one. With two lines crossing, the plane is split into four regions. Wait, let me draw that in my mind. Yes, l