 intersects C1 at A and C. So, AB is actually part of the line AC, with B between A and C.\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of C can be found by parametrizing the line AB beyond B. Let's parameterize the line AB. Let\u2019s use a parameter t where t=0 is at A and t=1 is at B. Then, to get beyond B, t > 1.\n\nBut maybe it's easier to parametrize the line AB. The parametric equations are:\n\nx = R + t*( (r\u00b2/R) - R ) = R + t*( (r\u00b2 - R\u00b2)/R )\n\ny = 0 + t*( r\u221a(R\u00b2 - r\u00b2)/R - 0 ) = t*( r\u221a(R\u00b2 - r\u00b2)/R )\n\nWe need to find another intersection point with C1, which is the circle x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 = R\u00b2.\n\nSubstitute x and y from the parametric equations into C1's equation:\n\n[ R + t*( (r\u00b2 - R\u00b2)/R ) ]\u00b2 + [ t*( r\u221a(R\u00b2 - r\u00b2)/R ) ]\u00b2 = R\u00b2\n\nExpand this:\n\n= [ R + t*( (r\u00b2 - R\u00b2)/R ) ]\u00b2 + [ t^2 * r\u00b2 (R\u00b2 - r\u00b2)/R\u00b2 ] = R\u00b2\n\nFirst, compute the first term:\n\n= [ R + (t (r\u00b2 - R\u00b2))/R ]\u00b2\n\n= R\u00b2 + 2 R * (t (r\u00b2 - R\u00b2)/R ) + (t (r\u00b2 - R\u00b2)/R )\u00b2\n\n= R\u00b2 + 2 t (r\u00b2 - R\u00b2) + t\u00b2 (r\u00b2 - R\u00b2)^2 / R\u00b2\n\nThe second term is t\u00b2 r\u00b2 (R\u00b2 - r\u00b2)/R\u00b2\n\nSo total equation:\n\nR\u00b2 + 2 t (r\u00b2 - R\u00b2) + t\u00b2 (r\u00b2 - R\u00b2)^2 / R\u00b2 + t\u00b2 r\u00b2 (R\u00b2 - r\u00b2)/R\u00b2 = R\u00b2\n\nSubtract R\u00b2 from both sides:\n\n2 t (r\u00b2 - R\u00b2) + t\u00b2 [ (r\u00b2 - R\u00b2)^2 + r\u00b2 (R\u00b2 - r\u00b2) ] / R\u00b2 = 0\n\nFactor out (R\u00b2 - r\u00b2) in the numerator of the second term:\n\n= 2 t (r\u00b2 - R\u00b2) + t\u00b2 (R\u00b2 - r\u00b2) [ (R\u00b2 - r\u00b2) - r\u00b2 ] / R\u00b2\n\nWait, let's compute the numerator inside the brackets:\n\n(r\u00b2 - R\u00b2)^2 + r\u00b2 (R\u00b2 - r\u00b2) = (R\u00b2 - r\u00b2)^2 + r\u00b2 (R\u00b2 - r\u00b2) = (R\u00b2 - r\u00b2)(R\u00b2 - r\u00b2 + r\u00b2) = (R\u00b2 - r\u00b2)(R\u00b2) = R\u00b2(R\u00b2 - r\u00b2