 $AB$ with $C_1$. Since we already know $D$ is the midpoint of $AB$, it follows that:\n   \n   \\[\n   D = \\left( \\frac{A + B}{2} \\right).\n   \\]\n\n3. **Properties of Tangents and Circle Intersection**\n\n   Notice that $AB$ is tangent to $C_2$ at $B$, which implies:\n   \n   \\[\n   AB \\cdot AC = AB^2.\n   \\]\n\n   This comes from the Power of a Point theorem applied to point $A$ regarding circle $C_1$.\n\n4. **Choosing Points $E$ and $F$**\n\n   A line through $A$ intersects $C_2$ at points $E$ and $F$. We need to show that the perpendicular bisectors of $DE$ and $CF$ intersect at a point $M$ on segment $AB$.\n\n5. **Perpendicular Bisectors and Cyclic Quadrilateral**\n\n   The assumption here is that $D, E, F, C$ are concyclic points. To show this, consider the power of point $O$ with respect to the concentric circles:\n   \n   \\[\n   OE \\cdot OF = r^2.\n   \\]\n   \n   Due to the symmetric property, every perpendicular bisector generated from any point on a line through the center intersects at $O$, confirming that $M$ lies on line $AB$.\n\n6. **Finding the Ratio \\( \\frac{AM}{MC} \\)**\n\n   Given the symmetry and properties of distances in a circle, we know that:\n   \n   \\(\n   AM = MB = MC\n   \\)\n   \n   Since $D$ is the midpoint of $[AB]$, we consider $M$ as the midpoint of $[DC]$ as $M$ lies on all perpendicular bisectors generated from the similar nature of intersections. Thus, the ratio is:\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AM}{MC} = 1\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\(\n\\boxed{1}\n\\)", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Given a right triangle \\( ABC \\) with legs \\( AC = a \\) and \\( CB = b \\). Find:\n\na) the side length of the largest square (with vertex \\( C \\)) that lies entirely within the triangle \\( ABC \\);\n\nb) the dimensions of the largest rectangle (with vertex \\( C \\)) that lies entirely within the triangle \\( ABC \\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I have this probl