ms:\n\\[ \\cos x + \\cos(x + \\frac{2\\pi}{3}) + \\cos(x + \\frac{4\\pi}{3}) \\]\nand\n\\[ \\sin x + \\sin(x + \\frac{2\\pi}{3}) + \\sin(x + \\frac{4\\pi}{3}). \\]\n\n#### Steps:\n1. **Finding \\(\\cos(x + \\frac{2\\pi}{3})\\) and \\(\\cos(x + \\frac{4\\pi}{3})\\):**\n   - Using the angle addition formula \\(\\cos(A + B) = \\cos A \\cos B - \\sin A \\sin B\\):\n     \\[\n     \\cos(x + \\frac{2\\pi}{3}) = \\cos x \\cos \\left( \\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\right) - \\sin x \\sin \\left( \\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\right).\n     \\]\n   - Since \\(\\cos \\left( \\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\right) = -\\frac{1}{2}\\) and \\(\\sin \\left( \\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\right) = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\), we get:\n     \\[\n     \\cos(x + \\frac{2\\pi}{3}) = \\cos x \\left( -\\frac{1}{2} \\right) - \\sin x \\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\right) = -\\frac{1}{2} \\cos x + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\sin x.\n     \\]\n   - Similarly:\n     \\[\n     \\cos(x + \\frac{4\\pi}{3}) = \\cos x \\cos \\left( \\frac{4\\pi}{3} \\right) - \\sin x \\sin \\left( \\frac{4\\pi}{3} \\right).\n     \\]\n   - Since \\(\\cos \\left( \\frac{4\\pi}{3} \\right) = -\\frac{1}{2}\\) and \\(\\sin \\left( \\frac{4\\pi}{3} \\right) = -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\), we get:\n     \\[\n     \\cos(x + \\frac{4\\pi}{3}) = \\cos x \\left( -\\frac{1}{2} \\right) - \\sin x \\left( -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\right) = -\\frac{1}{2} \\cos x - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\sin x.\n     \\]\n\n2. **Summing the cosine terms:**\n   \\[\n   \\cos x + \\cos(x + \\frac{2\\pi}{3}) + \\cos(x + \\frac{4\\pi}{3}) = \\cos x + \\left( -\\frac{1}{2} \\cos x + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\sin x \\right) + \\left( -\\frac{1}{2} \\cos x - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\sin x \\right).\n   \\]\n   Simplifying:\n   \\[\n   \\cos x - \\frac{1}{2} \\cos x - \\frac{1}{2} \\cos x + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\sin x - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\sin x = \\cos x - \\cos x = 0.\n   \\]\n   Hence:\n   \\[\n   \\cos x + \\cos(x + \\frac{2\\pi}{3}) + \\cos(x + \\frac{4\\pi}{3}) = 0.\n   \\]\n\n3. **Finding \\(\\sin(x + \\frac{2