 \\( f(\\pi) \\) be the smallest positive integer \\( i \\) such that \\(\\pi(1), \\pi(2), \\ldots, \\pi(i)\\) is a permutation of \\( 1, 2, \\ldots, i \\).\n   \n2. **Generalize for any \\( n \\):**\n   - Extend the definition of \\( f \\) to apply to any permutation of \\( 1, 2, \\ldots, n \\). This indicates \\( f(\\pi) \\) is the smallest \\( i \\) such that \\(\\pi(1), \\pi(2), \\ldots, \\pi(i)\\) is a permutation of \\( 1, 2, \\ldots, i \\) for any \\( n \\).\n\n3. **Define \\( g(n) \\):**\n   - Define \\( g(n) \\) to be the number of permutations \\(\\pi\\) of \\( 1, 2, \\ldots, n \\) such that \\( f(\\pi)=n \\). It means \\( g(n) \\) counts the permutations where the property holds for the entire set.\n\n4. **Base case:**\n   - \\( g(1) = 1 \\).\n\n5. **Recursive Formula:**\n   - For \\( n, k \\in \\mathbb{N} \\) with \\( k \\leq n \\), the number of permutations \\(\\pi\\) of \\( 1, 2, \\ldots, n \\) such that \\( f(\\pi) = k \\):\n     - is given by \\( g(k) \\times (n-k)! \\).\n   - This means:\n     \\[\n     g(n) = n! - \\sum_{k=1}^{n-1} g(k) \\times (n-k)!\n     \\]\n\n6. **Calculate values of \\( g(n) \\) for \\( n = 1, 2, \\ldots, 7 \\):**\n   - \\( g(1) = 1 \\)\n   - \\( g(2) = 2! - g(1) \\times 1! = 2 - 1 \\times 1 = 1 \\)\n   - \\( g(3) = 3! - (g(1) \\times 2! + g(2) \\times 1!) = 6 - (1 \\times 2 + 1 \\times 1) = 3 \\)\n   - \\( g(4) = 4! - (g(1) \\times 3! + g(2) \\times 2! + g(3) \\times 1!) = 24 - (1 \\times 6 + 1 \\times 2 + 3 \\times 1) = 13 \\)\n   - \\( g(5) = 5! - (g(1) \\times 4! + g(2) \\times 3! + g(3) \\times 2! + g(4) \\times 1!) = 120 - (1 \\times 24 + 1 \\times 6 + 3 \\times 2 + 13 \\times 1) = 71 \\)\n   - \\( g(6) = 6! - (g(1) \\times 5! + g(2) \\times 4! + g(3) \\times 3! + g(4) \\times 2! + g(5) \\times 1!) = 720 - (1 \\times 120 + 1 \\times 24 + 3 \\times 6 + 13 \\times 2 + 71 \\times 1) = 461 \\)\n   - \\( g(7) = 7! - (g(1) \\times 6! + g(2) \\times 5! + g(3) \\times 4! + g(4) \\times 3! + g(5) \\times 2! + g(6) \\times 1!) =