rm: total 3xy\n\n- y\u00b2: first term\n\n- yz: first term, second term, third term (zy = yz): total 3yz\n\n- xz: first term, third term (zx = xz): total 2xz\n\n- z\u00b2: second term\n\n- zx: second term, third term: total 2zx = 2xz\n\nWait, no. Wait, in the third term, zx is written as zx, which is same as xz. Wait, confusion arises due to notation.\n\nWait, let me list all terms:\n\n1. xy (from first term)\n\n2. y\u00b2 (from first term)\n\n3. yz (from first term)\n\n4. xz (from first term)\n\n5. yz (from second term)\n\n6. z\u00b2 (from second term)\n\n7. zx (from second term)\n\n8. yx (from second term) = xy\n\n9. zx (from third term)\n\n10. x\u00b2 (from third term)\n\n11. xy (from third term)\n\n12. zy (from third term) = yz\n\nSo, grouping:\n\n- xy: terms 1, 8, 11: 3xy\n\n- y\u00b2: term 2: 1y\u00b2\n\n- yz: terms 3,5,12: 3yz\n\n- xz: terms 4,7,9: 3xz (term 4: xz, term7: zx, term9: zx)\n\n- z\u00b2: term6: 1z\u00b2\n\n- x\u00b2: term10: 1x\u00b2\n\nTherefore, total sum is:\n\n3xy + y\u00b2 + 3yz + 3xz + z\u00b2 + x\u00b2.\n\nWhich can be rewritten as:\n\nx\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + z\u00b2 + 3xy + 3yz + 3xz.\n\nAh! So \u03a3 (x + y)(y + z) = x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + z\u00b2 + 3xy + 3yz + 3xz.\n\nNotice that x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + z\u00b2 + 3xy + 3yz + 3xz = (x + y + z)^2 + 2(xy + yz + zx). Wait:\n\n(x + y + z)^2 = x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + z\u00b2 + 2xy + 2yz + 2zx.\n\nSo, x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + z\u00b2 + 3xy + 3yz + 3xz = (x + y + z)^2 + (xy + yz + zx).\n\nTherefore, \u03a3 (x + y)(y + z) = (x + y + z)^2 + (xy + yz + zx).\n\nSo, going back to the Cauchy-Schwarz application:\n\nWe had (\u03a3 \u221a[3x(x + y)(y + z)])^2 \u2264 (\u03a3 3x)(\u03a3 (x + y)(y + z)) = 3(x + y + z)[(x + y + z)^2 + (xy + yz + zx)].\n\nSo, the RHS of the Cauchy-Schwarz gives 3(x + y + z)[(x + y + z)^2 + (xy + yz + zx)].\n\nWe need to compare this to [\u221a(4(x + y + z)^3)]^2 = 4(x + y + z)^3.\n\nTherefore, to prove that 3(x + y + z)[(x + y + z)^2 + (xy + yz + zx)] \u2264 4(x + y + z)^3.\n\nSimplify both sides:\n\nLeft side: 3(x + y + z)^3 + 3(x + y + z)(xy + yz + zx)\n\nRight side: 4(x + y + z)^3\n\nSubtract left side from right side:\n\n4(x + y + z)^3 - 3(x + y + z)^3 - 3(x + y + z)(xy + yz + zx) = (x + y + z)^3 - 3(x + y + z)(xy + yz + zx)\n\nFactor out (x + y + z):\n\n(x + y + z)[(x + y + z)^2 - 3(xy + yz + zx)]\n\nNow, compute (x + y + z)^2 - 3(xy + yz + zx):\n\n= x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + z\u00b2 + 2xy + 2yz + 2zx - 3xy - 3yz - 3zx\n\n= x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + z\u00b2 - xy - yz - zx\n\nTherefore, the difference is (x + y + z)(x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + z\u00b2 - xy - yz - zx)\n\nNow, since x, y, z > 0, x + y + z > 0. We need to check whether x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + z\u00b2 - xy - yz - zx is non-negative.\n\nBut x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + z\u00b2 - xy - yz - zx = 1/2[(x - y)^2 + (y - z)^2 + (z - x)^2] \u2265 0. This is a well-known identity.\n\nTherefore, the difference is non-negative, implying that 4(x + y + z)^3 \u2265 3(x + y + z)[(x + y + z)^2 + (xy + yz + zx)].\n\nHence, our earlier application of Cauchy-Schwarz gives:\n\n(\u03a3 \u221a[3x(x + y)(y + z)])^2 \u2264 3(x + y + z)[(x + y + z)^2 + (xy + yz + zx)] \u2264 4(x + y + z)^3.\n\nTherefore, taking square roots on both sides (since both sides are positive):\n\n\u03a3 \u221a[3x(x + y)(y + z)] \u2264 \u221a[4(x + y + z)^3]\n\nWhich is the desired inequality. Therefore, the proof is complete.\n\nBut let me check the equality condition. Since we applied Cauchy-Schwarz, equality holds when a_i / b_i is constant for all i. That is, \u221a[3x] / \u221a[(x + y)(y + z)] = \u221a[3y] / \u221a[(y + z)(z + x)] = \u221a[3z] / \u221a[(z + x)(x + y)].\n\nWhich simplifies to:\n\n\u221ax / \u221a[(x + y)(y + z)] = \u221ay / \u221a[(y + z)(z + x)] = \u221az / \u221a[(z + x)(x + y)].\n\nCancel out \u221a[(y + z)] from the first two terms:\n\n\u221ax / \u221a(x + y) = \u221ay / \u221a(z + x)\n\nCross-multiplying:\n\n\u221ax * \u221a(z + x) = \u221ay * \u221a(x + y)\n\nSquare both sides:\n\nx(z + x) = y(x + y)\n\n=> x z + x\u00b2 = x y + y\u00b2\n\n=> x\u00b2 - x y + x z - y\u00b2 = 0\n\nSimilarly, equate first and third ratios:\n\n\u221ax / \u221a[(x + y)(y + z)] = \u221az / \u221a[(z + x)(x + y)]\n\nCancel \u221a[(x + y)]:\n\n\u221ax / \u221a(y + z) = \u221az / \u221a(z + x)\n\nCross-multiply:\n\n\u221ax * \u221a(z + x) = \u221az * \u221a(y + z)\n\nSquare both sides:\n\nx(z + x) = z(y + z)\n\n=> x z + x\u00b2 = z y + z\u00b2\n\n=> x\u00b2 + x z - y z - z\u00b2 = 0\n\nFrom the first equality: x\u00b2 - x y + x z - y\u00b2 = 0\n\nFrom the second equality: x\u00b2 + x z - y z - z\u00b2 = 0\n\nAssuming equality holds, both equations must be satisfied.\n\nLet me suppose x = y = z. Then:\n\nFirst equation: x\u00b2 - x\u00b2 + x\u00b2 - x\u00b2 = 0, which holds.\n\nSecond equation: x\u00b2 + x\u00b2 - x\u00b2 - x\u00b2 = 0, which holds. So equality holds when x = y = z.\n\nAre there other cases? Suppose x \u2260 y \u2260 z.\n\nAssume x = y. Then:\n\nFirst equation: x\u00b2 - x\u00b2 + x z - x\u00b2 = x z - x\u00b2 = 0 => z = x.\n\nTherefore, equality holds only when x = y = z. Hence, the equality condition is x = y = z.\n\nTherefore, the proof via Cauchy-Schwarz gives the desired inequality with equality when x = y = z.", "deepseek_solution": "To prove the inequality \\( \\sum \\sqrt{3x(x+y)(y+z)} \\leqslant \\sqrt{4(x+y+z)^{3}} \\) for \\( x, y, z > 0 \\), we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1: Apply Cauchy-Schwarz Inequality**\n\nUsing the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality in the form \\( \\left( \\sum a_i b_i \\right)^2 \\leq \\left( \\sum a_i^2 \\right) \\left( \\sum b_i^2 \\right) \\), set:\n- \\( a_i = \\sqrt{3x} \\) and \\( b_i = \\sqrt{(x+y)(y+z)} \\) for each cyclic permutation.\n\n**Step 2: Compute the Sums**\n\nFirst, compute \\( \\sum a_i^2 \\) and \\( \\sum b_i^2 \\):\n- \\( \\sum a_i^2 = 3(x + y + z) \\).\n- \\( \\sum b_i^2 = \\sum (x+y)(y+z) \\).\n\nExpand \\( \\sum (x+y)(y+z) \\):\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sum (x+y)(y+z) &= (x+y)(y+z) + (y+z)(z+x) + (z+x)(x+y) \\\\\n&= x^2 + y^2 + z^2 + 3(xy + yz + zx).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n**Step 3: Apply Cauchy-Schwarz Bound**\n\nSubstitute into the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality:\n\\[\n\\left( \\sum \\sqrt{3x(x+y)(y+z)} \\right)^2 \\leq 3(x+y+z) \\left[ (x+y+z)^2 + (xy + yz + zx) \\right].\n\\]\n\n**Step 4: Compare to the Right-Hand Side (RHS)**\n\nThe squared RHS is \\( 4(x+y+z)^3 \\). Thus, we need to show:\n\\[\n3(x+y+z)\\left[ (x+y+z)^2 + (xy + yz + zx) \\right] \\leq 4(x+y+z)^3.\n\\]\n\nSimplify by subtracting \\( 3(x+y+z)^3 \\) from both sides:\n\\[\n3(x+y+z)(xy + yz + zx) \\leq (x+y+z)^3.\n\\]\n\nDivide both sides by \\( x+y+z \\) (positive):\n\\[\n3(xy + yz + zx) \\leq (x+y+z)^2.\n\\]\n\n**Step 5: Verify the Final Inequality**\n\nExpand \\( (x+y+z)^2 \\):\n\\[\nx^2 + y^2 + z^2 + 2(xy + yz + zx) \\geq 3(xy + yz + zx),\n\\]\nwhich simplifies to:\n\\[\nx^2 + y^2 + z^2 \\geq xy + yz + zx.\n\\]\nThis is always true since \\( x^2 + y^2 + z^2 - xy - yz - zx = \\frac{1}{2}[(x-y)^2 + (y-z)^2 + (z-x)^2] \\geq 0 \\).\n\n**Equality Condition**: Equality holds when \\( x = y = z \\), as verified by substituting back into the original inequality.\n\n**Final Conclusion**:\n\\[\n\\sum \\sqrt{3x(x+y)(y+z)} \\leqslant \\sqrt{4(x+y+z)^{3}}.\n\\]\nwith equality if and only if \\( x = y = z \\). \n\n\\(\\boxed{\\sum \\sqrt{3x(x+y)(y+z)} \\leqslant \\sqrt{4(x+y+z)^{3}}}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. Consider the given inequality:\n\n   \\[\n   \\sum \\sqrt{3 x(x+y)(y+z)} \\leq \\sqrt{4(x+y+z)^{3}}.\n   \\]\n\n2. Note the equivalence:\n\n   \\[\n   \\sum \\sqrt{3 x(x+y)(y+z)} = \\sum x \\sqrt{\\frac{3(x+y)(y+z)}{x}}.\n   \\]\n\n3. We need to prove the following inequality instead:\n\n   \\[\n   \\sum x \\sqrt{\\frac{3(x+y)(y+z)}{x}} \\leq \\sqrt{4(x+y+z)^{3}}.\n   \\]\n\n4. Assume \\( x+y+z=1 \\) (without loss of generality due to the homogeneity of the expression).\n\n5. Analyze the function \\( f(x) = \\sqrt{x} \\):\n\n   \\[\n   f'(x) = \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} > 0,\n   \\]\n   \n   \\[\n   f''(x) = -\\frac{1}{4x \\sqrt{x}} < 0.\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\( f(x) \\) is concave, we can apply the weighted Jensen's inequality.\n\n6. By Jensen's inequality:\n\n   \\[\n   \\sum x \\sqrt{\\frac{3(x+y)(y+z)}{x}} \\leq \\sqrt{\\sum x \\frac{3(x+y)(y+z)}{x}} = \\sqrt{3 \\sum (x+y)(y+z)}.\n   \\]\n\n7. It suffices to show:\n\n   \\[\n   3 \\sum (x+y)(y+z) \\leq 4(x+y+z)^3 = 4(1)^3 = 4.\n   \\]\n\n8. Compute \\( \\sum (x+y)(y+z) \\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\sum (x+y)(y+z) = \\sum (xy + xz + y^2 + yz).\n   \\]\n\n9. Simplify using \\( \\sum x = 1 \\):\n\n   \\[\n   3 \\sum (x+y)(y+z) = 3\\left( \\sum x^2 + 3 \\sum yz \\right).\n   \\]\n\n10. Using that \\( \\left( \\sum x \\right)^2 = 1^2 = 1 \\):\n\n    \\[\n    3\\left( \\sum x^2 + 3 \\sum yz \\right) = 3 \\left( 1 + \\sum yz \\right).\n    \\]\n\n11. We need to show:\n\n    \\[\n    3(1 + \\sum yz) \\leq 4.\n    \\]\n\n12. This is equivalent to proving that:\n\n    \\[\n    \\sum yz \\leq \\frac{1}{3}.\n    \\]\n\n13. By the Arithmetic Mean-Geometric Mean (AM-GM) inequality:\n\n    \\[\n    \\frac{xy + yz + zx}{3} \\leq \\left( \\frac{x+y+z}{3} \\right)^2 = \\left( \\frac{1}{3} \\right)^2 = \\frac{1}{9}.\n    \\]\n\n    Hence,\n\n    \\[\n    xy + yz + zx \\leq 3 \\times \\frac{1}{9} = \\frac{1}{3}.\n    \\]\n\n14. Since equality holds when \\( x = y = z = \\frac{1}{3} \\), we have shown the desired result.\n\nConclusion:\n\n\\(\\boxed{\\sum \\sqrt{3 x(x+y)(y+z)} \\leq \\sqrt{4(x+y+z)^{3}}.}\\)", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Initially, the numbers 3, 9, and 15 were written on a board. It was allowed to add any two of the recorded numbers, subtract the third number from that sum, and then write the result on the board in place of the number that was subtracted. After performing this operation multiple times, three numbers remained on the board, with the smallest being 2013. What were the other two numbers?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Alright, let's try to figure out this problem. So, we start with the numbers 3, 9, 