 2002 \\]\n\n1. **Rewrite the expression in grouped terms:**\n\nTo simplify the given expression, we can group consecutive terms in pairs as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 \\cdot 2 - 2 \\cdot 3 + 3 \\cdot 4 - 4 \\cdot 5 + \\cdots + 2001 \\cdot 2002.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n2. **Analyze the individual pairs:**\n\nFor each pair \\(n\\cdot(n+1) - (n+1)\\cdot(n+2)\\), observe:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nn \\cdot (n+1) - (n+1) \\cdot (n+2) = n \\cdot (n+1) - (n^2 + 3n + 2).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the above expression, we get:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nn(n+1) - (n^2 + 3n + 2) &= n^2 + n - (n^2 + 3n + 2) \\\\\n&= n^2 + n - n^2 - 3n - 2 \\\\\n&= -2n - 2 \\\\\n&= -2(n + 1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nSo each pair is simplified to: \\((n + 1) - (n + 1 + 1)\\).\n\n3. **Using the observed pattern, we generalize for all terms:**\n\nNow, we have the expression broken into:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n 2 - 3 + 3 \\cdot 4 - 4 \\cdot 5 + \\ldots + 2001 \\cdot 2002.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nRegrouped as:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 + (3 \\cdot (4-2)) + (3 \\cdot 4 - 3 \\cdot 2) + (5 \\cdot (6 - 4)) + \\ldots + (2001 \\cdot (2002-2000)).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 + 6 + 10 + \\ldots + 4002.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n4. **Identify the arithmetic sequence:**\n\nNotice that the numbers 2, 6, 10, ..., 4002 form an arithmetic sequence with the first term \\(a = 2\\) and the common difference \\(d = 4\\).\n\n5. **Calculate the number of terms:**\n\nThe general formula for the n-th term of an arithmetic sequence is:\n\\[\na_n = a + (n-1)d.\n\\]\nWe need the number of terms for \\(a_n = 4002\\):\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 + (n - 1) \\cdot 4 &= 4002 \\\\\n(n - 1) \\cdot 4 &= 4000 \\\\\nn - 1 &= 1000 \\\\\nn &= 1001.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThis sequence has 1001 terms.\n\n6. **Calculate the sum of the arithmetic sequence:**\n\nThe sum \\(S_n\\) of the first \\(n\\) terms of an arithmetic sequence is given by:\n\\[\nS_n = \\frac{n}{2} \\left(2a + (n-1