\n4. **Empty Space Calculation**:\n   - The empty space in the cylinder is the total volume of the cylinder minus the total volume of the spheres.\n   - Empty space = \\( 10\\pi r^3 - \\frac{20}{3}\\pi r^3 = \\frac{30}{3}\\pi r^3 - \\frac{20}{3}\\pi r^3 = \\frac{10}{3}\\pi r^3 \\).\n\n5. **Ratio of Empty Space to Occupied Space**:\n   - The ratio is calculated by dividing the empty space by the occupied space.\n   - Ratio = \\( \\frac{\\frac{10}{3}\\pi r^3}{\\frac{20}{3}\\pi r^3} = \\frac{10}{20} = \\frac{1}{2} \\).\n\nThus, the ratio of the empty space to the occupied space in the package is \\boxed{\\dfrac{1}{2}}.", "ground_truth_solution": "\n#### Step 1: Determine the volume occupied by a single sphere and its corresponding cylinder\n\nGiven that a sphere occupies exactly \\(\\frac{2}{3}\\) of the volume of the cylinder into which it is inscribed, we start by considering a single sphere with radius \\(R\\). The volume \\(V_{\\text{sphere}}\\) of the sphere is:\n\n\\[\nV_{\\text{sphere}} = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi R^3\n\\]\n\nFor the cylinder in which the sphere is inscribed, the height \\(h\\) and diameter \\(d\\) of the cylinder are both equal to \\(2R\\). The volume \\(V_{\\text{cylinder}}\\) of this cylinder is:\n\n\\[\nV_{\\text{cylinder}} = \\pi R^2 h = \\pi R^2 (2R) = 2 \\pi R^3\n\\]\n\n#### Step 2: Calculate the proportion of empty space within a single cylinder\n\nGiven the occupied volume ratio, we know that the sphere takes up \\(\\frac{2}{3}\\) of the cylinder's volume. Therefore, the volume of empty space \\(V_{\\text{empty}}\\) within the cylinder is:\n\n\\[\nV_{\\text{empty}} = V_{\\text{cylinder}} - V_{\\text{sphere}} = 2 \\pi R^3 - \\frac{4}{3} \\pi R^3\n\\]\n\nSimplifying, we find:\n\n\\[\nV_{\\text{empty}} = \\left(2 - \\frac{4}{3}\\right) \\pi R^3 = \\left(\\frac{6}{3} - \\frac{4}{3}\\right) \\pi R^3 = \\frac{2}{3} \\pi R^3\n\\]\n\n#### Step 3: Ratio of empty space to occupied volume in a single cylinder\n\nTo find the required ratio within a single 