tance between \\( O \\) and \\( O_1 \\) is equal to the sum of the semi-axes of the ellipse.\n\n2. Let \\( \\varphi \\) be the angle between one of the tangents and the \\( x \\)-axis.\n   \n3. Consider the circle \\( S \\) centered at \\(\\left((a+b) \\cos \\varphi, (a+b) \\sin \\varphi \\right)\\), which passes through the point \\( A = \\left( a \\cos \\varphi, b \\sin \\varphi \\right) \\), where \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) are the semi-major and semi-minor axes of the ellipse, respectively.\n\n4. The equation of the tangent to the ellipse at point \\( A \\) is:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{a \\cos \\varphi}{a^2} x + \\frac{b \\sin \\varphi}{b^2} y = 1\n   \\]\n\n5. Simplifying the equation of the tangent, we get:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\cos \\varphi}{a} x + \\frac{\\sin \\varphi}{b} y = 1\n   \\]\n\n6. This line is perpendicular to the line passing through \\( A \\) and \\( O_1 \\), whose equation is:\n   \\[\n   y = \\frac{a \\sin \\varphi}{b \\cos \\varphi} x + c\n   \\]\n   simplifying we get the slope \\( m = \\frac{a \\sin \\varphi}{b \\cos \\varphi} \\).\n\n7. The tangents \\( l_1 \\) and \\( l_2 \\) must touch the ellipse at some points, say \\( A \\) and \\( B \\). By utilizing the formula for the distance from a point to a line, we need to calculate the distance from the center \\( O \\) to the line \\( l_1 \\) or \\( l_2 \\).\n\n8. Let the tangent \\( l_1 \\) touch the ellipse at the point \\( (-a \\cos \\alpha, b \\sin \\alpha) \\). Then its equation is:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{-x \\cos \\alpha}{a} + \\frac{y \\sin \\alpha}{b} = 1\n   \\]\n\n9. Thus, we have:\n   \\[\n   \\tan \\varphi = \\frac{b \\cos \\alpha}{a \\sin \\alpha}\n   \\]\n\n10. The square of the distance from the origin to the line \\( l_1 \\) is:\n    \\[\n    d^2 = \\frac{b^2}{\\sin^2 \\alpha} \\cdot \\frac{1}{1 + \\tan^2 \\varphi} = b^2 \\frac{\\cos^2 \\varphi}{\\sin^2 \\alpha}\n    \\]\n\n11. Simplifying the expression, we obtain:\n    \\[\n    b^2 \\cos^2 \\varphi \\left( 1 + \\frac{a^2 \\sin^2 \\varphi}{b^2 \\cos^2 \\varphi} \\right) = b^2 \\cos^2 \\varphi + a^2 \\sin^2 \\varphi\n    \\]\n\n12. As a result, we have shown that the point \\( O_1 \\), the center of circle \\( S \\), indeed lies on the locus passing through the point where the tangents touch the ellipse, which matches the radius condition for tangency.\n\n13. Consequently, the distance \\( OO_1 \\) is equal to \\( a + b \\), the sum of the semi-major and semi-minor axes of the ellipse. \n\nConclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{a+b}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}]